Nice improvement, the two statements avoid one full copy of the context.

An optional idea, not blocking: part of the work could be decided at compile time.

1. With `only`, the context is known to be empty, so the generated code could be reduced to a single statement and skip the `$context = [];` assignment:

   ```php
   $context = $vars + $this->env->getGlobals();
   ```

   The precedence stays the same (with variables, then globals).

2. When the variables node is an empty `ArrayExpression` (`{% with {} %}`), `WithNode::compile()` could skip the `is_iterable()` check, `toArray()` and the context merge entirely, and only add the globals when `only` is set.

The second case is rare in real templates, and a variable that is empty at runtime cannot be detected by the compiler, so I would not expect a measurable gain there. The first case is cheap to implement and makes the generated code shorter.
